======================================================================================================================
1.  load the Baumgardt globular clusters
======================================================================================================================
  loaded 157 globular clusters with mass, half-mass radius, R_GC and central sigma
  [PASS] A1 the sample is genuinely baryon-dominated, which is the whole point: stellar mass-to-light ratios, no dark-matter fraction to confound the test the way there is for dwarf spheroidals   (median M/L_V = 1.87; no cluster carries a dark halo, so a kernel failure here is a pressure-support failure with the confound removed)

======================================================================================================================
2.  which clusters have ANY power?  internal acceleration vs the Milky Way external field
======================================================================================================================
     internal acceleration below a_0 (some deep-MOND interior): 35 of 157
     Milky Way external field below a_0 (isolated kernel applies): 25 of 157
     BOTH -- the only clusters that can see an isolated boost: 16 of 157
        Ter 8            R_GC=  20.4 kpc  g_int/a_0=0.125  g_ext/a_0=0.920
        Arp 2            R_GC=  21.4 kpc  g_int/a_0=0.077  g_ext/a_0=0.879
        NGC 7492         R_GC=  23.6 kpc  g_int/a_0=0.131  g_ext/a_0=0.797
        Pal 13           R_GC=  24.6 kpc  g_int/a_0=0.008  g_ext/a_0=0.765
        AM 4             R_GC=  24.6 kpc  g_int/a_0=0.004  g_ext/a_0=0.765
        Whiting 1        R_GC=  35.1 kpc  g_int/a_0=0.004  g_ext/a_0=0.535
        Pal 15           R_GC=  37.3 kpc  g_int/a_0=0.054  g_ext/a_0=0.504
        Pyxis            R_GC=  38.6 kpc  g_int/a_0=0.043  g_ext/a_0=0.487
        Sagittarius II   R_GC=  59.5 kpc  g_int/a_0=0.015  g_ext/a_0=0.316
        Pal 14           R_GC=  68.5 kpc  g_int/a_0=0.010  g_ext/a_0=0.274
        Eridanus         R_GC=  89.8 kpc  g_int/a_0=0.020  g_ext/a_0=0.209
        NGC 2419         R_GC=  95.9 kpc  g_int/a_0=0.839  g_ext/a_0=0.196
  [PASS] A2 (THE POWER LIMIT, up front) only a handful of outer-halo clusters reach the regime where the framework predicts anything but Newtonian internal dynamics; most are EFE-quenched or Newtonian-dense.  That the framework predicts Newton for them, and Newton is what they show, is a quiet success for the external-field effect but carries little discriminating power   (16 of 157 clusters have both a sub-a_0 interior and a sub-a_0 external field)

======================================================================================================================
3.  the prediction each arm makes for the discriminating clusters
======================================================================================================================
     discriminating clusters used: 16
     modified GRAVITY (EFE-quenched)  predicted/observed sigma: median 2.006  (+0.302 dex)
     modified INERTIA proxy (no EFE)  predicted/observed sigma: median 3.638  (+0.561 dex)
  [PASS] A3 (the confound-free result, and it is a mild OVER-prediction, not a deficit) with the dark-matter confound removed the framework does NOT sit on the diffuse outer-halo clusters: even with its external-field effect ON it OVER-predicts their dispersions by about 0.3 dex.  This is the known MOND tension for outer-halo globular clusters (Pal 14, Pal 4; Jordi+2009, Frank+2012, Baumgardt+), and here it is stated as what it is rather than tuned away -- the sign is OPPOSITE to the dwarf-spheroidal deficit, where the framework fell SHORT   (modified gravity (EFE on) over-predicts by +0.302 dex; a boost-free Newtonian interior would sit near 0, so the residual is the surviving MOND boost that the diffuse clusters do not show)
  [PASS] A4 (THE POLARITY, pointing the OTHER way from the dwarfs) the no-external-field limit -- the direction a modified-inertia arm with a weaker EFE would move -- OVER-predicts globular-cluster dispersions, because nothing quenches the boost.  Where the dwarf spheroidals appeared to favour modified inertia, the globular clusters disfavour it   (no-EFE over-predicts by +0.561 dex vs EFE-on +0.302 dex; the difference +0.259 dex IS the external-field effect and it is required to match)
  ⚠️ AGAINST INTEREST: (i) the discriminating sample is small and outer-halo clusters have the worst masses and
  largest tidal effects; (ii) the modified-inertia 'proxy' is the no-EFE limit, a CARICATURE not a real prediction
  -- modified inertia is a class with no unique EFE, so A4 is a DIRECTION not a number; (iii) real dispersions
  carry rotation, mass segregation and tidal heating the virial estimate ignores.  A4 is a lean, not a kill.

======================================================================================================================
4.  mutation control
======================================================================================================================
  [PASS] M1 mutation: pairing each cluster's predicted dispersion with a DIFFERENT cluster's observed one degrades the agreement, so A3 is per-cluster and not two overlapping distributions   (shuffled 0.370 dex vs matched 0.064 dex)

======================================================================================================================
VERDICT
======================================================================================================================
  Globular clusters are the pressure-supported systems with the dark-matter confound removed, and g03 died on that
  confound.  With it gone the framework does not sit on them either -- but it fails in the OPPOSITE direction from
  the dwarf spheroidals: on the diffuse outer-halo clusters it OVER-predicts the dispersion by about 0.3 dex even
  with the external-field effect on, the known MOND outer-halo-cluster tension, whereas on the dwarfs it fell short.
  Two things survive as real.  First, the external-field effect is doing genuine work: it cuts the over-prediction
  from 0.56 to 0.30 dex, so a modified-inertia arm with a weaker effect makes the clusters WORSE, the opposite of
  what the dwarfs suggested.  Second, and this is the honest headline, the pressure-supported systems do NOT speak
  with one voice: dwarfs under-predicted and lean toward modified inertia, clusters over-predict and lean back
  toward the external-field effect of modified gravity.  A single local modification of gravity that fits rotating
  discs is pulled in two directions at once by the two pressure-supported populations.
  Everything here is small-sample (16 discriminating clusters), the modified-inertia side is a caricature, and the
  virial estimate ignores rotation and tides -- so this SHARPENS the fork, it does not close it.  The confound-free
  statement that survives all of that: with dark matter removed as an explanation, pressure support ALONE is enough
  to break the framework's kernel, in both directions.

